facts:
A **broken (truncated) axis** on a bar graph can exaggerate relative bar heights and make small differences look much larger. A clearly labelled restricted axis can appropriately show variation in a line graph.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,6.0); \draw[->] (0,0)--(6.0,0);
\foreach \y/\lab in {0/68,1.5/70,3/72,4.5/74} {\draw (-0.15,\y)--(0,\y); \node[left,font=\small] at (-0.15,\y) {$\lab$};}
\draw (1.0,0) rectangle (2.2,1.5); \draw (3.2,0) rectangle (4.4,5.25);
\node[below,font=\small] at (1.6,0) {P}; \node[below,font=\small] at (3.8,0) {Q};
\node[rotate=90] at (-1.7,2.5) {Value};
\node[below] at (3.0,-1.0) {Item};
\node[font=\small] at (3.0,6.6) {axis starts at $68$: gap looks huge};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The same two values on an axis starting at $0$ show the true, much smaller gap.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,6.0); \draw[->] (0,0)--(6.0,0);
\foreach \y/\lab in {0/0,1.1/20,2.2/40,3.3/60,4.4/80} {\draw (-0.15,\y)--(0,\y); \node[left,font=\small] at (-0.15,\y) {$\lab$};}
\draw (1.0,0) rectangle (2.2,3.85); \draw (3.2,0) rectangle (4.4,4.125);
\node[below,font=\small] at (1.6,0) {P}; \node[below,font=\small] at (3.8,0) {Q};
\node[rotate=90] at (-1.7,2.5) {Value};
\node[below] at (3.0,-1.0) {Item};
\node[font=\small] at (3.0,6.6) {axis starts at $0$: same data, small gap};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.question_text:
A shop advert says Tuesday's sales were three times Monday's because its bar is three times as tall. Read the actual values, explain why the claim is misleading, and suggest a fairer display.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[black,->] (0,0)--(0,3.5);
\draw[black,->] (0,0)--(4.4,0);
\foreach \y/\v in {0/80,1/84,2/88,3/92} {\draw[black] (-0.1,\y)--(0,\y);\node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (-0.1,\y) {$\v$};}
\draw[black] (0.7,0) rectangle (1.6,1);
\draw[black] (2.5,0) rectangle (3.4,3);
\node[below] at (1.15,0) {Monday};
\node[below] at (2.95,0) {Tuesday};
\node[rotate=90] at (-0.85,1.75) {Items sold};
\node[above] at (2.2,3.6) {Shop sales};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
Monday sold $84$ items and Tuesday sold $92$ items.
$\begin{aligned}92-84&=8\\3\times84&=252\end{aligned}$
Tuesday sold only $8$ more items. Three times Monday's sales would be $252$, so the claim is false.
The vertical axis starts at $80$, exaggerating the difference between the bar heights. Start it at $0$, keep equal intervals and use equal-width bars.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[black,->] (0,0)--(0,3.5);
\draw[black,->] (0,0)--(4.4,0);
\foreach \y/\v in {0/0,0.8/25,1.6/50,2.4/75,3.2/100} {\draw[black] (-0.1,\y)--(0,\y);\node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (-0.1,\y) {$\v$};}
\draw[black] (0.7,0) rectangle (1.6,2.688);
\draw[black] (2.5,0) rectangle (3.4,2.944);
\node[below] at (1.15,0) {Monday};
\node[below] at (2.95,0) {Tuesday};
\node[above] at (1.15,2.688) {$84$};
\node[above] at (2.95,2.944) {$92$};
\node[rotate=90] at (-0.85,1.75) {Items sold};
\node[above] at (2.2,3.6) {Shop sales};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

